================================================================================
TRANSITION PHASE: PROJECTS -> SKILLS (The Shatter & Flood)

Vibe: Breaking point. The high-pressure aerodynamic gallery cannot withstand
the descent, resulting in a structural failure that plunges the user into
a deep-sea ecosystem.
Goal: Seamlessly transition the physics engine from 'Springs/Wind' to
'Fluid/Boids', and shift the scroll direction from vertical to horizontal.

SCROLL MECHANICS (The Directional Pivot)

This transition marks the boundary where standard vertical scrolling is
hijacked and mapped to a horizontal cinematic timeline.

Container Setup: A position: sticky pinning container wrapping the end
of the Projects section and the beginning of the Skills section.

The Pivot: As the user scrolls vertically past the final Project Card, the
viewport locks. Further downward mouse-wheeling no longer moves the page
up/down, but instead scrubs a timeline that drives the Shatter sequence
and subsequently pans the camera to the right (X-axis) into the Water section.

STAGE 1: PRESSURE BUILD (80% - 95% Scroll Progress of Projects)

Before the break, the UI warns the user of the impending depth.

The Vignette: The clean #F8FAFC (Light Slate) background begins to darken
at the extreme corners. A radial gradient vignette of #0A1128 (Deep Indigo)
creeps inwards.

Card Destabilization:

The spring physics on any remaining visible Project Cards begin to fail.

The cards undergo a rapid, low-amplitude vibration (simulating intense
pressure via requestAnimationFrame X/Y coordinate noise).

Visual Cue: A faint, jagged SVG "spiderweb" crack opacity fades in over the
top-level viewport glass (z-index 99).

STAGE 2: THE SHATTER (95% - 100% Scroll Progress)

The threshold is crossed. The "VR Headset / Gallery Glass" completely fails.

The Break:

An instant, full-screen Canvas particle effect is triggered.

The "glass" of the screen shatters into hundreds of sharp, polygonal
shards (Canvas polygons mapped with the #F8FAFC background color).

Gravity Shift:

The shards are subjected to heavy downward gravity, tumbling out of the
bottom of the viewport.

Any remaining DOM Project Cards immediately unmount or drop identically.

Audio/Haptic (Implied): A sharp, jarring visual flash of pure white (#FFFFFF)
for exactly 1 frame to sell the impact of the break.

STAGE 3: THE INK FLOOD & ORIENTATION SHIFT

The void left by the shattered glass is instantly consumed by water.

The Flood:

Directly behind the falling shards, the background is revealed to be pure
#0A1128 (Deep Indigo).

A subtle "ink drop" or fluid displacement shader (WebGL) ripples outward
from the center of the screen, distorting the falling shards as they sink.

Horizontal Initiation:

The user's vertical scroll input is now entirely mapped to horizontal
movement (transform: translateX()).

The camera begins its slow, stabilized cinematic pan to the right, revealing
the dark expanse of the deep sea ecosystem.

STAGE 4: PHYSICS HAND-OFF (Welcome to the Deep)

The transition of the cursor's role is completed.

Particle Metamorphosis: As the last glass shards sink out of frame, the
first "Boids" (glowing, bioluminescent dots representing your skills) swim
into the frame from the right side.

Cursor Transformation:

The cursor stops acting as an aerodynamic "wind draft" (from the Air section).

It becomes a soft, glowing, interactive fluid emitter. Moving the mouse
now leaves a faint, dissipating trail in the #0A1128 water, and acts as
"bait" that gently attracts the Boid particles.

TECHNICAL UX & PERFORMANCE NOTES

Shatter Engine: Do not attempt to shatter DOM elements. Use an overlay <canvas>.
Right before the shatter, take a snapshot of the UI (or just use solid geometric
shard shapes) and run a simple 2D particle explosion.

Scroll Hijacking: Use GSAP ScrollTrigger with pin: true. This handles the
complex math of locking the viewport, running the shatter timeline, and smoothly
translating Y-scroll to X-scroll without breaking native momentum scrolling
on Mac trackpads or mobile devices.